@0    = ~*Will! Are you leaving?*~
@1    = ~Yes. I've talked to <CHARNAME>. It seems that <PRO_HESHE> would welcome my help.~
@2    = ~*I understand. Good luck then, and remember about Visionhive. You may return whenever you want.*~
@3    = ~*Are you the one who banished the Shade Lord? The one who cleansed the altar at the ruined temple?*~
@4    = ~Uh, is it normal for you to be followed by some ghastly lights in the night? I think I'd like to know as I just got really close to having a heart attack!~
@5    = ~Yes. That would be me. What are you?~
@6    = ~I have no idea what are you talking about. My name is Bunnyslug and I'm a merchant. I sell hammers. Hammers and... some other tools.~
@7    = ~*Are you sure you're not the one I'm looking for? I can sense your energy. It's strong and bitter. And that hearbeat - it's potent.*~
@8    = ~Fine, fine. Yes, I'm the one who killed the Shade Lord. And you are...?~
@9    = ~I told you - I'm someone else. Begone!~
@10   = ~*Your kind call us 'will-o'-wisps'. I've been searching for you. I... we need someone of strong body and mind. A being different to others. You cleansed the temple of Amaunator, changed the forest around it... but you can still do so much more.*~
@11   = ~So, abbil, are we going to listen to some dusty souls lost long ago? Waste no time for the dead, <CHARNAME>, as there are dozens of living ones who would have your head first.~
@12   = ~Are you truly going to speak with this undead thing, <CHARNAME>? It should be cleansed, not talked to.~
@13   = ~Be cautious when in the presence of spectres like this, <CHARNAME>, this may be a trap of sorts.~
@14   = ~I think I saw a wisp like this in the forest, <CHARNAME>, but it had always kept a fair distance. My mother once told me tales about wisps. They may be vile, so you'd best be careful.~
@15   = ~This light... I can sense it's cold and dead, but... it's beautiful, isn't it? Perhaps it's not going to attack?~
@16   = ~Ah, the witch's light - a wandering spirit! Even in Kozakura we have tales of such beings, <CHARNAME>, most peculiar tales. Although they may pose a threat, there is surely no harm in listening, yes?~
@17   = ~Ah, the luring light! So, my raven, are we going to chance fate and allow it speak? There is a part of me, my friend, that would like to gamble with death itself - I beseech you, my dear leader, hear its words. I am most curious.~
@18   = ~This light is a dead thing, <CHARNAME>. Its existence violates the greater balance. Be careful, as it may have even more sinister plans.~
@19   = ~Hmm, I remember my grand-grand-grand father telling me stories about wisps like this. Oh, there was one particular tale that I distinctly remember: one about a grave, a pond and a field of carrots. Or maybe that was something else... no, carrots. They were definitely carrots. So, anyways, every night someone saw one of these lights, it would mean someone near the field or the pond would disappear! Er, I can't be certain if it was because of the light... people could easily fall into the pond since there were some roots near it, but... well, the conclusion is that people would rather blame these wisps. They call it a bad omen, you see.~
@20   = ~Ya know, I've seen many strange things with you, but this light... it's a bit creepier than most. We haven't been really lucky, lately, so let's be careful about it, okay?~
@21   = ~My father told me about these creatures. Most stories would draw them as evil creatures, but sometimes they aren't that bad. We should let it speak. It hasn't tried to attack us yet.~
@22   = ~What would you ask of me, wisp? Why were you searching for me?~
@23   = ~Begone, creature, or die! The choice is yours to make!~
@24   = ~*The place you know as the ruined temple of Amaunator once pulsed with primal energy... a place once thriving with spirits. It was once a great grove, but then came the followers of Amaunator that claimed our land. Although they did not destroy the sacred grove, it became weaker after their presence. But even then, we still had our protectors, our shamans. Even at its weakest, there was always a spark of life in the roots and walls.*~
@25   = ~*What's more, there was always hope. We have never forgotten, we have nurtured what we owned. After you defeated the Shade Lord, we knew this was our chance.*~
@26   = ~*Now that he's gone, we may try to bring back the full power of our grotto. Help us. All we want is to have our beloved sanctuary back. Our place. Our home.*~
@27   = ~You cannot truly be considering this, <CHARNAME>, could you?~
@28   = ~Think carefully, <CHARNAME>. Will-o'-wisps are seldom of good intent, but if these ones wish to restore the long gone balance, then it may be worth considering. I will not tell you what to do, but I will follow you no matter what you decide.~
@29   = ~Boo says that not all spirits are evil! Rashemen is full of spirits, too, and if some balance can be brought back, we should help! And if some evil comes to stop us, it will have its butt kicked! Right, Boo? *squeak*~
@30   = ~And what would be my role in your plan, wisp?~
@31   = ~You're asking the right person. As a shaman, I felt something in that place. Now, after what you've said, all has become clear.~
@32   = ~I think I might help you, but I will need some details. What would you expect me to do, spirit?~
@33   = ~I refuse to help you, ghastly light. Begone!~
@34   = ~*I knew you were the right person!*~
@35   = ~*There is a bag with the ashes of our protector, the last shaman who took care of our grotto. Ashes that must be strewn in the cave to the west of Amaunator's temple. But...*~
@36   = ~...but?~
@37   = ~It can't be that easy. There must be something else. Am I right?~
@38   = ~*The bag was stolen. A thief took it and fled to the village on the hills. The human settlement, but name of it I do not know.*~
@39   = ~The Umar Hills? I think I know the village you're talking about.~
@40   = ~You must be talking of Imnesvale. Is the thief still there?~
@41   = ~I'm sorry, but I'm not going to help you, wisp. Begone.~
@42   = ~*You know it? Of course... it must be they who sent you here. Shadows devoured their people. That's why they asked you for help. Now it is us who would ask you for aid.*~
@43   = ~I'll help you.~
@44   = ~I'm not going to help you, creature. Begone.~
@45   = ~*I know not. Please, find him.*~
@46   = ~Fine. I'll look for him.~
@47   = ~No. Now begone, spirit!~
@48   = ~*I thank you. When you find the ashes, please bring it to the cave and place it in the pond. It will bring the cave to its former glory and restore its power. You can take whatever else the cave and plants treasured in its sand and roots.*~
@49   = ~(The spirit levitates in silence for a moment and then disappears into the air)~
@50   = ~*You've done it! I can feel the life flowing back through the roots and energy returning to the pond! I am happy to present to you the Visionhive - our beloved grotto, our long missed shamanic sanctuary.*~
@51   = ~Some powerful magic has just awoken, <CHARNAME>. I suppose you have developed a certain habit of doing this.~
@52   = ~I must admit, <CHARNAME> - it is majestic.~
@53   = ~Oh, Boo can smell magic here! Do you like it, my brave hamster?~
@54   = ~*squeak* Boo says he likes it!~
@55   = ~'Tis a wonderful grotto, my raven, and somehow I feel satisfied that thanks to our act, these roots and magic live again. And that spectacle - aye, a peculiar and powerful display it was. Bravo.~
@56   = ~Are we done here, abbil? Do what you must and let us leave this place and focus on more important things.~
@57   = ~Is it really the same cave? It looks so... different.~
@58   = ~You might have made a mistake restoring this place, <CHARNAME>. Its power will no doubt attract evil spirits or demons. But the decision has been made.~
@59   = ~Well, that was a show certainly worth our attention, <CHARNAME>. And if there's any matter that needs our attention in these woods, now we have our own hideout.~
@60   = ~That was quite pretty, don't you think?~
@61   = ~Well, that was strange - even for me. I just hope those roots won't try to catch or smash us or something.~
@62   = ~I can sense the life returning to this grove. Well done, <CHARNAME>.~
@63   = ~By Arvoreen, what a surprise. I would never have expected this place to change so much. I have a feeling that the change is for good.~
@64   = ~Bah! We should have leeched the grotto's magic when we still could! (Such a waste of magical energy...)~
@65   = ~Why would you help some worthless ghosts, <CHARNAME>? How much time are you planning to waste on these dead things?~
@66   = ~I could feel how the cave reshaped itself. Never have I felt something like that.~
@67   = ~I hope ye know whet yer doin', <CHARNAME>. Don't get us into som' damn mess. These woods are strange enough without som' bloody ghosts!~
@68   = ~My aunt would never believe me if I told her what I've seen today. This sort of thing is much more exciting than back at the Keep.~
@69   = ~*Thank you for what you've done. You may take everything you can find here, in the grotto. Our kind and the grotto itself will allow you. But... you should get more for your efforts.*~
@70   = ~*There, just behind you - there lies a skin. Perhaps you'll find a way to make a use of it.*~
@71   = ~What? A skin? That's not what I have expected.~
@72   = ~It was an honour to help you in restoring that incredible place.~
@73   = ~Oh! A skin! Well... uhm... I'm sure I'll find a way to use it somehow. I think.~
@74   = ~*I am sure you can do something with it. It's naga skin. It may be powerful. And as I said - you may take whatever you find here.*~
@75   = ~*It is incredible, isn't it? I am glad I managed to find you.*~
@76   = ~*It's naga skin. I believe it may be worth something an experienced craftsman, if it is reshaped... just as this place was.*~
@77   = ~*Know that you're a fine adventurer. The other spirits tell me that you will grow even stronger, even more potent than you already are. After all, you are special. We know it.*~
@78   = ~*But before you leave, I have one last thing to say... you have proven yourself to be a powerful shaman, a person of great knowledge and power. The Visionhive can sense it, as can we all.~
@79   = ~You understand our kind well, and perhaps... you may wish to become our new protector, our eyes and ears in the world of mortal and in the land of the spirits. If you choose to accept, this the Visionhive can become your new home. You may visit us whenever you wish. If there is a matter that needs your attention here, you'll sense it. Are you willing to become one with us?*~
@80   = ~That's an interesting offer. Yes, I can imagine making this place my home. I can protect it.~
@81   = ~I'm sorry, but I'm too busy. I can't take care of you or this cave.~
@82   = ~*We understand and accept your answer.*~
@83   = ~*I'm so glad! We need time to heal our wounds and become even greater. We'll inform you when there is a matter worth your attention, shaman.*~
@84   = ~*Go now. We - the spirits - will observe you and hope for your victory.*~
@85   = ~*It is you! We hoped you'd arrive soon. There is a matter that needs your attention, shaman. Some ghosts of unknown origin sensed the restoration of the grotto's powers and sent an emissary to speak with you. I allowed him to enter, but his clan is not allowed to follow him unless you decide otherwise.~
@86   = ~They would like to negotiate a truce with us. Speak with him and decide if they can be trusted. Unfortunately... from what I can tell, their the emissary is very old fashioned. Remember to be wary of your words. He awaits you in the next cave.*~
@87   = ~*So he decided to attack again, but you managed to extinguish his existance.*~
@88   = ~At least we're finally done with him. I am glad you ended his existance, <CHARNAME>. He was a great pain.~
@89   = ~*You saw through his lies. You are indeed wise and powerful, shaman, just like our previous protectors.*~
@90   = ~Spirits like him can never win. He should have known I would stop him eventually.~
@91   = ~A pity you did not help me.~
@92   = ~Did you know about his plan?~
@93   = ~*That is why you're our protector, <CHARNAME>.*~
@94   = ~*Forgive me, but I'm no fighter. My light is old, and much less intense than years long past.*~
@95   = ~*No. I had a feeling -- but it was little more than a mere guess.*~
@96   = ~*This ghost - he was one of the Eidolon Mists. Centuries ago, he tried to poison Crytanner, one of your protectors. Their goal was to claim the grotto and make it their own, to feed on its powers. But Crytanner was powerful and cleansed her body of the toxin they infected her with and banished the Mists.*~
@97   = ~*I thought that perhaps after centuries, they would have learned from their mistakes. I thought they left their pride behind or exchanged it for wisdom. But I was wrong. Please, forgive me. Perhaps I should have never let him in.*~
@98   = ~It's fine. I am the protector of this grotto. It's my duty to listen and decide, not yours. You did well.~
@99   = ~Be more careful next time, wisp.~
@100  = ~*Thank you. But I will try to be a better support from now on, master shaman. You have protected our grotto well today, <CHARNAME>. I doubt the Mists will ever return. They know they are no match to you.*~
@101  = ~*I will. Again - forgive me. You have protected our grotto well today, <CHARNAME>. I doubt the Mists will ever return. They know they are no match to you.*~
@102  = ~*So there is a truce after all. I am happy to see that they've changed. I know little of the past, but once, long ago, his people tried to take the control of this place. They finally realized they have no chance of victory against our wise protectors.*~
@103  = ~I hope they've really changed, and this whole truce isn't a lie.~
@104  = ~If I understood them, they've had much time to think about what they have done.~
@105  = ~*I hope so, too, master shaman. Perhaps they realized how wrong they were to attack Crytanner.*~
@106  = ~*Yes. Some stories say they were once allies of the Visionhive. Perhaps their attempt to the the control was but a mistake, or a moment of greed. Perhaps they realized their error, after all those years.*~
@107  = ~*Also, I would like to thank you again that you came, master shaman. I can see you understand how important you are to this grotto and to us - the spirits.*~
@108  = ~I want to be a fine protector. I'm not going to leave. Nor you, nor the Visionhive.~
@109  = ~Heh, it was a nice break from my usual missions.~
@110  = ~*You're a supreme shaman and a perfect protector.*~
@111  = ~*I understand. Thank you again, master. Return whenever you want.*~
@112  = ~*So their desire for peace was a lie after all. I'm sorry I thought otherwise, master. I shouldn't have let him in when he claimed he wanted to see you.*~
@113  = ~*You're back, shaman. I hoped you would hear our calling. The Day of the Bitter Moss comes.*~
@114  = ~Ah, it's that time already? I suppose I have lost my sense of time.~
@115  = ~The Day of what?~
@116  = ~I'm not familiar with it. Could you please tell me what that is?~
@117  = ~*The Day of the Bitter Moss is the time when grotto shows miracles, a cheerful day for the spirits and all other beings that reside in this cave. But it can happen only if the master of the grotto performs a special ritual. The day is close now and that is why I called you, protector. We need to start the preparations.*~
@118  = ~It's a truly wonderful day, <CHARNAME>. You may believe me on that.~
@119  = ~I'll help with the preparations. What do we need?~
@120  = ~I value tradition. How should I prepare?~
@121  = ~I'm sorry, but I don't have time for this.~
@122  = ~*We need some objects. A dencrawler spider's web, a vilecrow mushroom, a metal bowl and some tigertooth grapes. When you gather all these items, put the bowl and all the other objects on the stone, here - between the ponds. If everything is there, the grotto shall channel the energy through your body and start the ritual.*~
@123  = ~I have a question before I leave.~
@124  = ~I don't have time for this. I refuse to do the ritual.~
@125  = ~Fine. I'll go to look for those items now.~
@126  = ~*Yes?*~
@127  = ~Where can I find the dencrawler spider's web?~
@128  = ~Where can I find the vilecrow mushroom?~
@129  = ~Where can I find the metal bowl?~
@130  = ~Where can I find the tigertooth grapes?~
@131  = ~Remind me why should I do with those items once I find them.~
@132  = ~I'll go and search for those components.~
@133  = ~*One of the spirits told me that they've seen a dencrawler spider near the entrance to the ruined temple.*~
@134  = ~*All the mushrooms were tainted with shadows, but perhaps someone in the village will have a fresh one? They are edible by your kind, after all.*~
@135  = ~*We have the bowl. You can find it in the chest, here in the Visionhive.*~
@136  = ~*Perhaps somewhere in the village? There are no grapes in these woods, sadly.*~
@137  = ~*Put everything on the rock. Over there, next to the tree.*~
@138  = ~*I... am shocked. If that is the case, you may resign and leave the Visionhive. You can come back, but not as our shaman. I am sorry. Do you still want to refuse to prepare for the ritual?*~
@139  = ~I didn't know it's that important. Fine, I can help you.~
@140  = ~Yes. I refuse.~
@141  = ~*Yes! I will wait for your return, protector.*~
@142  = ~*I see. I shall bother you no more.*~
@143  = ~*It always feels so good to see the cave alive, blooming with energy. Thank you for performing this ritual, shaman.*~
@144  = ~*Hello, <CHARNAME>. I assume you sensed my calling? There are no urgent issues this time, but we have a visitor. A merchant spirit has decided to stay at our grotto. He will stay here for three days before leaving. I thought that perhaps you'd like to look at his goods.*~
@145  = ~*Ah, you came! <CHARNAME>, this time it's something very serious.*~
@146  = ~Yes? What happened?~
@147  = ~Eh, what is it...?~
@148  = ~*An ancient spirit - Ghastlycleaver - is trying to attack the Visionhive. Thanks to your rituals, it cannot enter, but...*~
@149  = ~But...?~
@150  = ~Spit it out, wisp. I have no time to waste.~
@151  = ~*I... I think you have become stronger than previous shamans. Your powers, experience, your energy - I think you will be able to erase that being.*~
@152  = ~*Ghastlycleaver was born in another time. Some believe it was created from the energy of all banished spirits that once existed in this part of the Realms. It has but one goal - to destroy sacred places like this one, along with everything that's bound with it. It will want to kill us both as well, simply because we served the Visionhive.*~
@153  = ~*It won't rest until this place is destroyed. It will try to break the barriers first. We have enough energy to keep him outside, but his very existance is an insult to our grotto. And even though we are powerful, one day, when we are weaker, it may break in.*~
@154  = ~Ghastlycleaver... this monster must be stopped, <CHARNAME>.~
@155  = ~As much as I despise this grotto, <CHARNAME>, foul creatures like this Ghastlycleaver shouldn't be left free.~
@156  = ~So, should we prepare for another epic battle, my friend? Perhaps we should fill this cave with some traps? In case a rat decided to pay us a visit.~
@157  = ~Well, the thought of this fight gives my some goosebumps, but... we'll face him eventually, right? No matter what else we need to do, you're the one responsible for this place.~
@158  = ~Oh, great! Another wacky and gruesome thing is trying to hunt us? Okay, if we must do this, let me prepare my most powerful spells first. Aaaand let's hope we won't explode, along with this whole cave...~
@159  = ~I have never heard of this monster, <CHARNAME>, but if it is as the spirit claims, it is a threat to the balance. We should stop it.~
@160  = ~If you decide to protect this grotto, <CHARNAME>, I'll stand on your side. These woods have seen enough terrors.~
@161  = ~Eh, do we really have to fight this thing? It's pointless! (We should have burned this grotto down long time ago, anyway...)~
@162  = ~Eternals being such as this never give up, <CHARNAME>. Believe me.~
@163  = ~This creature - if it's truly as powerful as the spirit claims, it may be a threat not only to this grotto, <CHARNAME>, but to many innocent people. We should not ignore the matter. I support going and destroying it.~
@164  = ~Minsc and Boo are not afraid of any monsters! If he wants to mess with us, we should all kick its butt!~
@165  = ~Ghastlycleaver? I admit, my raven, that I thought creatures like that live only in tales and songs. For the Doomguard, we should challenge our fates once again and face it.~
@166  = ~This creature - it's not our problem. Not yet. But if you want to risk it -- well, that's your choice, abbil.~
@167  = ~We shouldn't let this... Ghastlycleaver attack when we're not ready. I... I think we should fight him on our terms - even though I find it hard to even imagine such a... thing existing.~
@168  = ~Hmm, the thought of fighting such a powerful being is more than tempting. If you decide to kill it, make sure to take me with you, <CHARNAME>. I want to hear it scream.~
@169  = ~That thing may be dangerous, <CHARNAME>, but the Moonmaiden will grant us power to defeat it.~
@170  = ~<CHARNAME>, what if this thing break the barrier and not only attack the Visionhive, but Imnesvale?! We can't let it happen!~
@171  = ~How would you suggest to deal with this thing?~
@172  = ~Do you even have a plan, or are you just considering letting this thing in to try and kill us?!~
@173  = ~*Ghastlycleaver does not belong to this place. I can let it in, and the grotto will make him slower, perhape even weaker. I think that's our only chance. You can also set some traps around the area. Prepare yourself. It is we who decide when it may enter.*~
@174  = ~*I can see no other option. Think about it and come back when you are ready.*~
@175  = ~*Are you ready for your fight with Ghastlycleaver? Know that for the time of the fight I will become one with the cave. I will weaken it, but not physically. You won't see me here, for I will make sure the grotto won't get damaged.*~
@176  = ~I'm ready for this. Let him in.~
@177  = ~Not yet. I'll come back to you when I'm ready.~
@178  = ~*Fine. Please, be careful and show no mercy to that thing.*~
@179  = ~*You... you really did it. You killed the monster! You are the greatest of our shamans, <CHARNAME>. Thanks to you, this grotto is safe and powerful. It has become a gem in the world of spirits.*~
@180  = ~That was my task from the very beginning, wasn't it?~
@181  = ~Yeah, right. I know.~
@182  = ~Whatever. I did this because I like testing my own limits, not because of some spirit cave.~
@183  = ~*And you did more than well. I thank you for everything.*~
@184  = ~*I'm so glad that thing is gone. Thank you. Thank you for everything, master shaman.*~
@185  = ~*No matter what your motives were, you have shown us the power you use in our defense. Thank you for everything, master shaman.*~
@186  = ~*All of these tasks have proven that you are more than just an adventurer. You are more than just some <PRO_RACE> - you know the way of spirits. You understand the visible and invisible energies and you were able to bind with the Visionhive. With the death of Ghastlycleaver, the grotto has become even more powerful. What's more, we need not fear anything within these walls.*~
@187  = ~*We owe all of this to you, shaman. We know that there are also other matters that echoes in your mind, which is why from we will try to take care of everything on our own from now on. But this place is still your home. Come whenever you want. You will always be our master shaman. Our protector.*~
@188  = ~*Is that a spider, shaman? Did you buy it?*~
@189  = ~Well... yes, but I thought it would be something else. But... yes, it's a spider. I have a spider. In a cave. Full of spirits. Well, that's strange.~
@190  = ~Yes. I bought it from the merchant who visited the Visionhive.~
@191  = ~Uhm. No, I--I think it came from the forest.~
@192  = ~*Ah--*~
@193  = ~...~
@194  = ~*I... I think it likes it here. What should we do with it, master shaman?*~
@195  = ~Hello, spider. So, you like this cave, do you, you eight legged cutie?~
@196  = ~Can you understand me, spider?~
@197  = ~(The spider looks at you for a while - it seems interested in you and the Visionhive; he first looks at the main tree, and then again at you.)~
@198  = ~Oh, so you like the tree, yes?~
@199  = ~Well, it's getting more and more peculiar.~
@200  = ~(It stares at you - it seems the spider would like to stay in the Visionhive.)~
@201  = ~Can he stay here, wisp?~
@202  = ~I won't let you stay here. Get out! Out of my home!~
@203  = ~*That depends on you. If you want, you may let it stay.*~
@204  = ~You want to let it stay? I find it foolish, but it is your choice to make.~
@205  = ~Fine, spider. You may stay.~
@206  = ~I don't want arachnids in my cave. Out! Out of my cave, spider!~
@207  = ~(The spider looks happy - after a moment it draws something in a pile of sand; spiders aren't well known from their artistic talents, but you may guess it's meant to resemble a fly.)~
@208  = ~Fine. If I find any nice flies, I will bring it.~
@209  = ~Okay, I'm not going to continue talking to a spider. You may stay, but we won't be friends. I'm not sure if you understand the concept of friendship, anyway...~
@210  = ~(It seems even happier.)~
@211  = ~(It makes a strange sound and the starts to ignore you.)~
@212  = ~(It leaves the cave.)~
@213  = ~(The spider approaches you and takes the fly you have found in the Underdark and eats it - you have never seen the spider so happy.)~
@214  = ~I think you just made a new friend, <CHARNAME>.~
@215  = ~(After a moment it finishes, takes something from between the rocks near the northern wall and gives it to you - it's some kind of glowing stone.)~
@216  = ~(The spider just looks at you - it seems to really like it in here.)~
@217  = ~*It's good to see you, <CHARNAME>. Is there anything you need?*~
@218  = ~I thought this place would be more occupied. Why don't I see that many spirits?~
@219  = ~How often will I need to return here?~
@220  = ~Is there anything you need from me?~
@221  = ~Goodbye.~
@222  = ~*They are here, shaman. However, as we have not had a guardian for a long time, many of my fellow wisps are... reluctant to reveal themselves to the living once more.~
@223  = ~*Perhaps some, like our eager storyteller, are curious enough to show themselves to you, and will have many things to say.~
@224  = ~*Perhaps once every few days. Do not worry yourself, shaman. We will attend to small matters such as passing spirits or the defense of the grotto ourselves. You will know if we desperately require your presence.*~
@225  = ~*No, not at the moment. We will contact you whenever something important happens.*~
@226  = ~*Is there anything else you need, shaman?*~
